#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:72
msgid "Basics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:74
msgid "How to do it?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:76
msgid ""
"Do we need to merge functions? The obvious answer is: Yes, that is quite a "
"possible case. We usually *do* have duplicates and it would be good to get "
"rid of them. But how do we detect duplicates? This is the idea: we split "
"functions into smaller bricks or parts and compare the \"bricks\" amount. If "
"equal, we compare the \"bricks\" themselves, and then do our conclusions "
"about functions themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:83
msgid ""
"What could the difference be? For example, on a machine with 64-bit pointers "
"(let's assume we have only one address space), one function stores a 64-bit "
"integer, while another one stores a pointer. If the target is the machine "
"mentioned above, and if functions are identical, except the parameter type "
"(we could consider it as a part of function type), then we can treat a "
"`uint64_t` and a `void*` as equal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:90
msgid ""
"This is just an example; more possible details are described a bit below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:92
msgid ""
"As another example, the reader may imagine two more functions. The first "
"function performs a multiplication by 2, while the second one performs a "
"logical left shift by 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:96
msgid "Possible solutions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:98
msgid ""
"Let's briefly consider possible options about how and what we have to "
"implement in order to create full-featured functions merging, and also what "
"it would mean for us."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:102
msgid ""
"Equal function detection obviously supposes that a \"detector\" method to be "
"implemented and latter should answer the question \"whether functions are "
"equal\". This \"detector\" method consists of tiny \"sub-detectors\", which "
"each answers exactly the same question, but for function parts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:107
msgid ""
"As the second step, we should merge equal functions. So it should be a "
"\"merger\" method. \"Merger\" accepts two functions *F1* and *F2*, and "
"produces *F1F2* function, the result of merging."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:111
msgid ""
"Having such routines in our hands, we can process a whole module, and merge "
"all equal functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:114
msgid ""
"In this case, we have to compare every function with every another function. "
"As the reader may notice, this way seems to be quite expensive. Of course we "
"could introduce hashing and other helpers, but it is still just an "
"optimization, and thus the level of O(N\\*N) complexity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:119
msgid ""
"Can we reach another level? Could we introduce logarithmical search, or "
"random access lookup? The answer is: \"yes\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:122
msgid "Random-access"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:124
msgid ""
"How can this be done? Just convert each function to a number, and gather all "
"of them in a special hash table. Functions with equal hashes are equal. Good "
"hashing means, that every function part must be taken into account. That "
"means we have to convert every function part into some number, and then add "
"it into the hash. The lookup-up time would be small, but such an approach "
"adds some delay due to the hashing routine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:131
msgid "Logarithmical search"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:133
msgid ""
"We could introduce total ordering among the functions set, once ordered we "
"could then implement a logarithmical search. Lookup time still depends on N, "
"but adds a little of delay (*log(N)*)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:137
msgid "Present state"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:139
msgid ""
"Both of the approaches (random-access and logarithmical) have been "
"implemented and tested and both give a very good improvement. What was most "
"surprising is that logarithmical search was faster; sometimes by up to 15%. "
"The hashing method needs some extra CPU time, which is the main reason why "
"it works slower; in most cases, total \"hashing\" time is greater than total "
"\"logarithmical-search\" time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:146
msgid "So, preference has been granted to the \"logarithmical search\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:148
msgid ""
"Though in the case of need, *logarithmical-search* (read \"total-ordering\") "
"could be used as a milestone on our way to the *random-access* "
"implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:151
msgid ""
"Every comparison is based either on the numbers or on the flags comparison. "
"In the *random-access* approach, we could use the same comparison algorithm. "
"During comparison, we exit once we find the difference, but here we might "
"have to scan the whole function body every time (note, it could be slower). "
"Like in \"total-ordering\", we will track every number and flag, but instead "
"of comparison, we should get the numbers sequence and then create the hash "
"number. So, once again, *total-ordering* could be considered as a milestone "
"for even faster (in theory) random-access approach."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:160
msgid "MergeFunctions, main fields and runOnModule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:162
msgid "There are two main important fields in the class:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:164
msgid ""
"`FnTree` – the set of all unique functions. It keeps items that couldn't be "
"merged with each other. It is defined as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:167
msgid "`std::set<FunctionNode> FnTree;`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:169
msgid ""
"Here, `FunctionNode` is a wrapper for `llvm::Function` class, with an "
"implemented “\\<” operator among the functions set (below we explain how it "
"works exactly; this is a key point in fast functions comparison)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:173
msgid ""
"`Deferred` – merging process can affect bodies of functions that are in "
"`FnTree` already. Obviously, such functions should be rechecked again. In "
"this case, we remove them from `FnTree`, and mark them to be rescanned, "
"namely put them into `Deferred` list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:178
msgid "runOnModule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:180
msgid "The algorithm is pretty simple:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:182
msgid "Put all module's functions into the *worklist*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:184
msgid ""
"Scan *worklist*'s functions twice: first, enumerate only strong functions "
"and then only weak ones:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:187
msgid ""
"2\\.1. Loop body: take a function from *worklist* (call it *FCur*) and try "
"to insert it into *FnTree*: check whether *FCur* is equal to one of "
"functions in *FnTree*. If there *is* an equal function in *FnTree* (call it "
"*FExists*): merge function *FCur* with *FExists*. Otherwise, add the "
"function from the *worklist* to *FnTree*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:193
msgid ""
"Once the *worklist* scanning and merging operations are complete, check the "
"*Deferred* list. If it is not empty, refill the *worklist* contents with "
"*Deferred* list and redo step 2, if the *Deferred* list is empty, then exit "
"from method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:198
msgid "Comparison and logarithmical search"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:200
msgid ""
"Let's recall our task: for every function *F* from module *M*, we have to "
"find equal functions *F\\`* in the shortest time possible and merge them "
"into a single function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:204
msgid ""
"Defining total ordering among the functions set allows us to organize "
"functions into a binary tree. The lookup procedure complexity would be "
"estimated as O(log(N)) in this case. But how do we define *total-ordering*?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:208
msgid ""
"We have to introduce a single rule applicable to every pair of functions, "
"and following this rule, then evaluate which of them is greater. What kind "
"of rule could it be? Let's declare it as the \"compare\" method that returns "
"one of 3 possible values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:213
msgid "-1, left is *less* than right,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:215
msgid "0, left and right are *equal*,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:217
msgid "1, left is *greater* than right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:219
msgid ""
"Of course, it means that we have to maintain *strict and non-strict order "
"relation properties*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:222
msgid "reflexivity (`a <= a`, `a == a`, `a >= a`),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:223
msgid "antisymmetry (if `a <= b` and `b <= a` then `a == b`),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:224
msgid "transitivity (`a <= b` and `b <= c`, then `a <= c`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:225
msgid "asymmetry (if `a < b`, then `a > b` or `a == b`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:227
msgid ""
"As mentioned before, the comparison routine consists of \"sub-comparison-"
"routines\", with each of them also consisting of \"sub-comparison-"
"routines\", and so on. Finally, it ends up with a primitive comparison."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:232
msgid "Below, we will use the following operations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:234
msgid ""
"`cmpNumbers(number1, number2)` is a method that returns -1 if left is less "
"than right; 0, if left and right are equal; and 1 otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:236
msgid ""
"`cmpFlags(flag1, flag2)` is a hypothetical method that compares two flags. "
"The logic is the same as in `cmpNumbers`, where `true` is 1, and `false` is "
"0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:240
msgid ""
"The rest of the article is based on *MergeFunctions.cpp* source code (found "
"in *\\<llvm_dir>/lib/Transforms/IPO/MergeFunctions.cpp*). We would like to "
"ask the reader to keep this file open, so we could use it as a reference for "
"further explanations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:245
msgid "Now, we're ready to proceed to the next chapter and see how it works."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:247
msgid "Functions comparison"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:249
msgid "First, let's define exactly how we compare complex objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:251
msgid ""
"Complex object comparison (function, basic-block, etc) is mostly based on "
"its sub-object comparison results. It is similar to the next \"tree\" "
"objects comparison:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:255
msgid ""
"For two trees *T1* and *T2* we perform *depth-first-traversal* and have two "
"sequences as a product: \"*T1Items*\" and \"*T2Items*\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:257
msgid ""
"We then compare chains \"*T1Items*\" and \"*T2Items*\" in the most-"
"significant-item-first order. The result of items comparison would be the "
"result of *T1* and *T2* comparison itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:261
msgid "FunctionComparator::compare(void)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:263
msgid ""
"A brief look at the source code tells us that the comparison starts in the "
"“`int FunctionComparator::compare(void)`” method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:266
msgid ""
"The first parts to be compared are the function's attributes and some "
"properties that are outside the “attributes” term, but still could make the "
"function different without changing its body. This part of the comparison is "
"usually done within simple *cmpNumbers* or *cmpFlags* operations (e.g. "
"`cmpFlags(F1->hasGC(), F2->hasGC())`). Below is a full list of function's "
"properties to be compared on this stage:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:273
msgid ""
"*Attributes* (those are returned by `Function::getAttributes()` method)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:275
msgid ""
"*GC*, for equivalence, *RHS* and *LHS* should be both either without *GC* or "
"with the same one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:277
msgid ""
"*Section*, just like a *GC*: *RHS* and *LHS* should be defined in the same "
"section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:279
msgid ""
"*Variable arguments*. *LHS* and *RHS* should be both either with or without "
"*var-args*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:281
msgid "*Calling convention* should be the same."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:283
msgid ""
"Function type. Checked by `FunctionComparator::cmpType(Type*, Type*)` "
"method. It checks return type and parameters type; the method itself will be "
"described later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:287
msgid ""
"Associate function formal parameters with each other. Then comparing "
"function bodies, if we see the usage of *LHS*'s *i*-th argument in *LHS*'s "
"body, then, we want to see usage of *RHS*'s *i*-th argument at the same "
"place in *RHS*'s body, otherwise functions are different. On this stage we "
"grant the preference to those we met later in function body (value we met "
"first would be *less*). This is done by “`FunctionComparator::"
"cmpValues(const Value*, const Value*)`” method (will be described a bit "
"later)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:295
msgid "Function body comparison. As written in method comments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:297
msgid ""
"“We do a CFG-ordered walk since the actual ordering of the blocks in the "
"linked list is immaterial. Our walk starts at the entry block for both "
"functions, then takes each block from each terminator in order. As an "
"artifact, this also means that unreachable blocks are ignored.”"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:302
msgid ""
"So, using this walk, we get BBs from *left* and *right* in the same order, "
"and compare them by “`FunctionComparator::compare(const BasicBlock*, const "
"BasicBlock*)`” method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:306
msgid ""
"We also associate BBs with each other, like we did it with function formal "
"arguments (see `cmpValues` method below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:309
msgid "FunctionComparator::cmpType"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:311
msgid "Consider how type comparison works."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:313
msgid ""
"Coerce pointer to integer. If the left type is a pointer, try to coerce it "
"to the integer type. It could be done if its address space is 0, or if "
"address spaces are ignored at all. Do the same thing for the right type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:317
msgid ""
"If the left and right types are equal, return 0. Otherwise, we need to give "
"preference to one of them. So proceed to the next step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:320
msgid ""
"If the types are of different kind (different type IDs). Return result of "
"type IDs comparison, treating them as numbers (use `cmpNumbers` operation)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:323
msgid ""
"If the types are vectors or integers, return result of their pointers "
"comparison, comparing them as numbers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:326
msgid ""
"Check whether type ID belongs to the next group (call it equivalent-group):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:328
msgid "Void"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:329
msgid "Float"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:330
msgid "Double"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:331
msgid "X86_FP80"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:332
msgid "FP128"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:333
msgid "PPC_FP128"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:334
msgid "Label"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:335
msgid "Metadata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:337
msgid ""
"If ID belongs to group above, return 0. Since it's enough to see that types "
"has the same `TypeID`. No additional information is required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:340
msgid ""
"Left and right are pointers. Return result of address space comparison "
"(numbers comparison)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:343
msgid ""
"Complex types (structures, arrays, etc.). Follow complex objects comparison "
"technique (see the very first paragraph of this chapter). Both *left* and "
"*right* are to be expanded and their element types will be checked the same "
"way. If we get -1 or 1 on some stage, return it. Otherwise, return 0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:348
msgid ""
"Steps 1-6 describe all the possible cases, if we passed steps 1-6 and didn't "
"get any conclusions, then invoke `llvm_unreachable`, since it's quite an "
"unexpectable case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:352
msgid "cmpValues(const Value\\*, const Value\\*)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:354
msgid "Method that compares local values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:356
msgid ""
"This method gives us an answer to a very curious question: whether we could "
"treat local values as equal, and which value is greater otherwise. It's "
"better to start from example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:360
msgid ""
"Consider the situation when we're looking at the same place in left function "
"\"*FL*\" and in right function \"*FR*\". Every part of *left* place is equal "
"to the corresponding part of *right* place, and (!) both parts use *Value* "
"instances, for example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:370
msgid "So, now our conclusion depends on *Value* instances comparison."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:372
msgid ""
"The main purpose of this method is to determine the relation between such "
"values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:374
msgid ""
"What can we expect from equal functions? At the same place, in functions "
"\"*FL*\" and \"*FR*\" we expect to see *equal* values, or values *defined* "
"at the same place in \"*FL*\" and \"*FR*\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:378
msgid "Consider a small example here:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:392
msgid ""
"In this example, *pf0* is associated with *pg0*, *pf1* is associated with "
"*pg1*, and we also declare that *pf0* < *pf1*, and thus *pg0* < *pf1*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:395
msgid ""
"Instructions with opcode \"*instr0*\" would be *equal*, since their types "
"and opcodes are equal, and values are *associated*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:398
msgid ""
"Instructions with opcode \"*instr1*\" from *f* is *greater* than "
"instructions with opcode \"*instr1*\" from *g*; here we have equal types and "
"opcodes, but \"*pf1* is greater than \"*pg0*\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:402
msgid ""
"Instructions with opcode \"*instr2*\" are equal, because their opcodes and "
"types are equal, and the same constant is used as a value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:405
msgid "What we associate in cmpValues?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:407
msgid ""
"Function arguments. *i*-th argument from left function associated with *i*-"
"th argument from right function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:409
msgid ""
"BasicBlock instances. In basic-block enumeration loop we associate *i*-th "
"BasicBlock from the left function with *i*-th BasicBlock from the right "
"function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:412
msgid "Instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:413
msgid ""
"Instruction operands. Note, we can meet *Value* here we have never seen "
"before. In this case it is not a function argument, nor *BasicBlock*, nor "
"*Instruction*. It is a global value. It is a constant, since it's the only "
"supposed global here. The method also compares: Constants that are of the "
"same type and if right constant can be losslessly bit-casted to the left "
"one, then we also compare them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:420
msgid "How to implement cmpValues?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:422
msgid ""
"*Association* is a case of equality for us. We just treat such values as "
"equal, but, in general, we need to implement antisymmetric relation. As "
"mentioned above, to understand what is *less*, we can use order in which we "
"meet values. If both values have the same order in a function (met at the "
"same time), we then treat values as *associated*. Otherwise, it depends on "
"who was first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:429
msgid ""
"Every time we run the top-level compare method, we initialize two identical "
"maps (one for the left side, another one for the right side):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:432
msgid "`map<Value, int> sn_mapL, sn_mapR;`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:434
msgid ""
"The key of the map is the *Value* itself; the *value* – is its order (call "
"it *serial number*)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:437
msgid "To add value *V* we need to perform the next procedure:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:439
msgid "`sn_map.insert(std::make_pair(V, sn_map.size()));`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:441
msgid ""
"For the first *Value*, the map will return *0*, for the second *Value*, the "
"map will return *1*, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:444
msgid ""
"We can then check whether the left and right values met at the same time "
"with a simple comparison:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:447
msgid "`cmpNumbers(sn_mapL[Left], sn_mapR[Right]);`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:449
msgid "Of course, we can combine insertion and comparison:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:458
msgid "Let's look at how the whole method could be implemented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:460
msgid ""
"We have to start with the bad news. Consider function self and cross-"
"referencing cases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:472
msgid ""
"This comparison has been implemented in initial *MergeFunctions* pass "
"version. But, unfortunately, it is not transitive. And this is the only case "
"we can't convert to less-equal-greater comparison. It is a seldom case, 4-5 "
"functions of 10000 (checked in test-suite), and, we hope, the reader would "
"forgive us for such a sacrifice in order to get the O(log(N)) pass time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:478
msgid ""
"If left/right *Value* is a constant, we have to compare them. Return 0 if it "
"is the same constant, or use `cmpConstants` method otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:481
msgid ""
"If left/right is *InlineAsm* instance. Return result of *Value* pointers "
"comparison."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:484
msgid ""
"Explicit association of *L* (left value) and *R* (right value). We need to "
"find out whether values met at the same time, and thus are *associated*. Or "
"we need to put the rule: when we treat *L* < *R*. Now it is easy: we just "
"return the result of numbers comparison:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:498
msgid ""
"Now, when *cmpValues* returns 0, we can proceed with the comparison "
"procedure. Otherwise, if we get (-1 or 1), we need to pass this result to "
"the top level, and finish comparison procedure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:502
msgid "cmpConstants"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:504
msgid "Performs a constant comparison as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:506
msgid ""
"Compare constant types using `cmpType` method. If the result is -1 or 1, "
"goto step 2, otherwise proceed to step 3."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:509
msgid ""
"If types are different, we still can check whether constants could be "
"losslessly bitcasted to each other. The further explanation is modification "
"of `canLosslesslyBitCastTo` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:513
msgid ""
"2\\.1 Check whether constants are of the first class types "
"(`isFirstClassType` check):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:516
msgid ""
"2\\.1.1. If both constants are *not* of the first class type: return result "
"of `cmpType`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:519
msgid ""
"2\\.1.2. Otherwise, if left type is not of the first class, return -1. If "
"right type is not of the first class, return 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:522
msgid ""
"2\\.1.3. If both types are of the first class type, proceed to the next step "
"(2.1.3.1)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:525
msgid ""
"2\\.1.3.1. If types are vectors, compare their bitwidth using the "
"*cmpNumbers*. If result is not 0, return it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:528
msgid "2\\.1.3.2. Different types, but not vectors:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:530
msgid "if both of them are pointers, good for us, we can proceed to step 3."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:531
msgid ""
"if one of types is pointer, return result of *isPointer* flags comparison "
"(*cmpFlags* operation)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:533
msgid ""
"otherwise we have no methods to prove bitcastability, and thus return result "
"of types comparison (-1 or 1)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:536
msgid ""
"Steps below are for the case when types are equal, or case when constants "
"are bitcastable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:539
msgid ""
"One of constants is a \"*null*\" value. Return the result of `cmpFlags(L-"
">isNullValue, R->isNullValue)` comparison."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:542
msgid "Compare value IDs, and return result if it is not 0:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:549
msgid ""
"Compare the contents of constants. The comparison depends on the kind of "
"constants, but on this stage it is just a lexicographical comparison. Just "
"see how it was described in the beginning of \"*Functions comparison*\" "
"paragraph. Mathematically, it is equal to the next case: we encode left "
"constant and right constant (with similar way *bitcode-writer* does). Then "
"compare left code sequence and right code sequence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:556
msgid "compare(const BasicBlock\\*, const BasicBlock\\*)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:558
msgid "Compares two *BasicBlock* instances."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:560
msgid "It enumerates instructions from left *BB* and right *BB*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:562
msgid ""
"It assigns serial numbers to the left and right instructions, using "
"`cmpValues` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:565
msgid ""
"If one of left or right is *GEP* (`GetElementPtr`), then treat *GEP* as "
"greater than other instructions. If both instructions are *GEPs* use "
"`cmpGEP` method for comparison. If result is -1 or 1, pass it to the top-"
"level comparison (return it)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:570
msgid ""
"3\\.1. Compare operations. Call `cmpOperation` method. If result is -1 or 1, "
"return it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:573
msgid "3\\.2. Compare number of operands, if result is -1 or 1, return it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:575
msgid ""
"3\\.3. Compare operands themselves, use `cmpValues` method. Return result if "
"it is -1 or 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:578
msgid ""
"3\\.4. Compare type of operands, using `cmpType` method. Return result if it "
"is -1 or 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:581
msgid "3\\.5. Proceed to the next instruction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:583
msgid "We can finish instruction enumeration in 3 cases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:585
msgid ""
"4\\.1. We reached the end of both left and right basic-blocks. We didn't "
"exit on steps 1-3, so contents are equal, return 0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:588
msgid "4\\.2. We have reached the end of the left basic-block. Return -1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:590
msgid "4\\.3. Return 1 (we reached the end of the right basic block)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:592
msgid "cmpGEP"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:594
msgid "Compares two GEPs (`getelementptr` instructions)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:596
msgid ""
"It differs from regular operations comparison with the only thing: "
"possibility to use `accumulateConstantOffset` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:599
msgid ""
"So, if we get constant offset for both left and right *GEPs*, then compare "
"it as numbers, and return comparison result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:602
msgid "Otherwise, treat it like a regular operation (see previous paragraph)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:604
msgid "cmpOperation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:606
msgid "Compares instruction opcodes and some important operation properties."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:608
msgid "Compare opcodes, if it differs return the result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:609
msgid "Compare number of operands. If it differs – return the result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:611
msgid ""
"Compare operation types, use *cmpType*. All the same – if types are "
"different, return result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:614
msgid ""
"Compare *subclassOptionalData*, get it with `getRawSubclassOptionalData` "
"method, and compare it like a numbers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:617
msgid "Compare operand types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:619
msgid ""
"For some particular instructions, check equivalence (relation in our case) "
"of some significant attributes. For example, we have to compare alignment "
"for `load` instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:623
msgid "O(log(N))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:625
msgid ""
"Methods described above implement order relationship. And latter, could be "
"used for nodes comparison in a binary tree. So we can organize functions set "
"into the binary tree and reduce the cost of lookup procedure from O(N\\*N) "
"to O(log(N))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:630
msgid "Merging process, mergeTwoFunctions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:632
msgid ""
"Once *MergeFunctions* detects that current function (*G*) is equal to one "
"that was analyzed before (function *F*) it calls "
"`mergeTwoFunctions(Function*, Function*)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:636
msgid ""
"Operation affects `FnTree` contents in the following way: *F* will stay in "
"`FnTree`. *G* being equal to *F* will not be added to `FnTree`. Calls of *G* "
"would be replaced with something else. It changes bodies of callers. So, "
"functions that calls *G* would be put into `Deferred` set and removed from "
"`FnTree`, and analyzed again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:642
msgid "The approach is as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:644
msgid ""
"Most wished case: when we can use alias and both of *F* and *G* are weak. We "
"make both of them with aliases to the third strong function *H*. Actually "
"*H* is *F*. See below how it's made (but it's better to look straight into "
"the source code). Well, this is a case when we can just replace *G* with *F* "
"everywhere, we use `replaceAllUsesWith` operation here (*RAUW*)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:650
msgid ""
"*F* could not be overridden, while *G* could. It would be good to do the "
"next: after merging the places where overridable function were used, still "
"use overridable stub. So try to make *G* alias to *F*, or create overridable "
"tail call wrapper around *F* and replace *G* with that call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:655
msgid ""
"Neither *F* nor *G* could be overridden. We can't use *RAUW*. We can just "
"change the callers: call *F* instead of *G*. That's what "
"`replaceDirectCallers` does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:659
msgid "Below is a detailed body description."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:661
msgid "If “F” may be overridden"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:663
msgid ""
"As follows from `mayBeOverridden` comments: “whether the definition of this "
"global may be replaced by something non-equivalent at link time”. If so, "
"that's ok: we can use alias to *F* instead of *G* or change call "
"instructions itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:667
msgid "HasGlobalAliases, removeUsers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:669
msgid ""
"First, consider the case when we have global aliases of one function name to "
"another. Our purpose is to make both of them with aliases to the third "
"strong function. However, if we keep *F* alive and without major changes, we "
"can leave it in `FnTree`. Try to combine these two goals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:674
msgid "Do a stub replacement of *F* itself with an alias to *F*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:676
msgid ""
"Create stub function *H*, with the same name and attributes like function "
"*F*. It takes maximum alignment of *F* and *G*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:679
msgid ""
"Replace all uses of function *F* with uses of function *H*. It is a two-step "
"procedure instead. First of all, we must take into account that all "
"functions that call *F* would be changed because we change the call argument "
"(from *F* to *H*). If so, we must review these caller functions again after "
"this procedure. We remove callers from `FnTree`, method with name "
"`removeUsers(F)` does that (don't confuse with `replaceAllUsesWith`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:686
msgid ""
"2\\.1. `Inside removeUsers(Value* V)` we go through the all values that use "
"value *V* (or *F* in our context). If value is instruction, we go to "
"function that holds this instruction and mark it as to-be-analyzed-again "
"(put to `Deferred` set), we also remove caller from `FnTree`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:692
msgid "2\\.2. Now we can do the replacement: call `F->replaceAllUsesWith(H)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:694
msgid ""
"*H* (that now \"officially\" plays *F*'s role) is replaced with alias to "
"*F*. Do the same with *G*: replace it with alias to *F*. So finally "
"everywhere *F* was used, we use *H* and it is alias to *F*, and everywhere "
"*G* was used we also have alias to *F*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:699
msgid "Set *F* linkage to private. Make it strong :-)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:701
msgid "No global aliases, replaceDirectCallers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:703
msgid ""
"If global aliases are not supported, we call `replaceDirectCallers`. Just go "
"through all calls of *G* and replace it with calls of *F*. If you look into "
"the method you will see that it scans all uses of *G* too, and if use is "
"callee (if user is call instruction and *G* is used as what to be called), "
"we replace it with use of *F*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:709
msgid "If “F” could not be overridden, fix it!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:711
msgid ""
"We call `writeThunkOrAlias(Function *F, Function *G)`. Here we try to "
"replace *G* with an alias to *F* first. The next conditions are essential:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:714
msgid "target should support global aliases,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:715
msgid ""
"the address itself of *G* should be not significant, not named and not "
"referenced anywhere,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:717
msgid "function should come with external, local or weak linkage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:719
msgid ""
"Otherwise, we write thunk: some wrapper that has *G's* interface and calls "
"*F*, so *G* could be replaced with this wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:722
msgid "*writeAlias*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:724
msgid "As follows from *llvm* reference:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:726
msgid ""
"“Aliases act as *second name* for the aliasee value”. So we just want to "
"create a second name for *F* and use it instead of *G*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:729
msgid "create global alias itself (*GA*),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:731
msgid ""
"adjust alignment of *F* so it must be maximum of current and *G's* alignment;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:733
msgid "replace uses of *G*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:735
msgid ""
"3\\.1. first mark all callers of *G* as to-be-analyzed-again, using "
"`removeUsers` method (see chapter above),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:738
msgid "3\\.2. call `G->replaceAllUsesWith(GA)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:740 ../../../MergeFunctions.md:756
msgid "Get rid of *G*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:742
msgid "*writeThunk*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:744
msgid "As it written in method comments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:746
msgid ""
"“Replace G with a simple tail call to bitcast(F). Also replace direct uses "
"of G with bitcast(F). Deletes G.”"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:749
msgid ""
"In general, it does the same as usual when we want to replace callee, except "
"the first point:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:752
msgid ""
"We generate tail call wrapper around *F*, but with an interface that allows "
"using it instead of *G*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MergeFunctions.md:755
msgid "“As-usual”: `removeUsers` and `replaceAllUsesWith` then."
msgstr ""
